VALUES

-- Q1: easy / L1 — SOC for Cybersecurity uses AICPA Description Criteria
(5, 'SOC Engagements', 'Which criteria framework does a SOC for Cybersecurity engagement use to evaluate an organization''s cybersecurity risk management program?', '["Trust Services Criteria categories from SOC 2 reporting", "AICPA Description Criteria and Control Criteria", "COSO Internal Control Integrated Framework only", "NIST Cybersecurity Framework exclusively"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): Per the AICPA SOC for Cybersecurity guide, this engagement uses the AICPA''s Description Criteria for the cybersecurity risk management program description and Control Criteria for evaluating controls. Unlike SOC 2, which uses Trust Services Criteria, SOC for Cybersecurity has its own dedicated criteria framework. Wrong (A): Trust Services Criteria are used by SOC 2, whereas SOC for Cybersecurity uses a separate AICPA-developed criteria framework. Wrong (C): Although COSO may inform controls, it is not the criteria framework specified for SOC for Cybersecurity engagements. Wrong (D): While organizations may reference NIST, the engagement itself uses AICPA Description Criteria and Control Criteria rather than NIST exclusively.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — SOC for Supply Chain audience and purpose
(5, 'SOC Engagements', 'Hawthorne Electronics manufactures circuit boards for 120 medical device companies. Several customers and their supply chain risk teams request independent assurance over Hawthorne''s production controls, component sourcing, and finished goods distribution. Which SOC engagement addresses this need?', '["A SOC 1 Type II report covering financial reporting controls", "A SOC 2 Type II report covering security and availability", "A SOC for Supply Chain report covering production and distribution", "A SOC for Cybersecurity report covering the IT environment"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): Per the AICPA SOC for Supply Chain guide, this engagement is designed for producing or manufacturing organizations to report on production and distribution controls. Hawthorne''s customers need assurance over manufacturing processes, whereas SOC 1 and SOC 2 address different assurance objectives. Wrong (A): SOC 1 addresses controls relevant to user entities'' internal control over financial reporting rather than manufacturing production and distribution. Wrong (B): SOC 2 evaluates IT-related controls against Trust Services Criteria, however Hawthorne''s customers need assurance over physical manufacturing and supply chain processes. Wrong (D): SOC for Cybersecurity evaluates entity-wide cybersecurity, unlike SOC for Supply Chain which covers production and distribution controls.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — System description five boundary components
(5, 'SOC Engagements', 'Oakmont Technology is preparing for its first SOC 2 Type II engagement. The IT director drafts a system description that covers the company''s cloud servers, network equipment, and firewall configurations. The service auditor reviews the draft and requests that three additional boundary components be documented. Which components are missing?', '["Software, people, and procedures", "Revenue streams, customer contracts, and pricing models", "Financial statements, tax filings, and audit opinions", "Marketing materials, press releases, and investor decks"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): Per AICPA SOC 2 guidance, a system description must address five components defining the system boundary: infrastructure, software, people, procedures, and data. Oakmont''s draft covers infrastructure elements (servers, network, firewalls), whereas the missing components are software, people, and procedures (with data also implied). Wrong (B): Revenue streams, customer contracts, and pricing models are business documents rather than system description boundary components. Wrong (C): Financial statements and audit opinions are not required in a SOC 2 system description, unlike the five boundary components specified by AICPA guidance. Wrong (D): Although marketing materials may describe services, they are not boundary components required in the system description.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — SOC engagement classification as attestation
(5, 'SOC Engagements', 'Redstone Financial Services hires a CPA firm to perform a SOC 2 Type II examination. The CEO asks whether the engagement follows the same professional standards as the company''s annual financial statement audit. Which statement correctly classifies the SOC engagement?', '["It follows AU-C auditing standards, the same as a financial statement audit", "It follows AT-C attestation standards, which differ from AU-C auditing standards", "It follows consulting standards with no formal opinion issued", "It follows agreed-upon procedures standards with findings reported to management"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): Per AT-C 205, SOC 2 engagements are examination-level attestation engagements governed by AT-C attestation standards. Unlike a financial statement audit conducted under AU-C, a SOC engagement results in an opinion on management''s assertion about the system description and controls. Wrong (A): SOC engagements follow AT-C attestation standards rather than AU-C auditing standards used for financial statement audits. Wrong (C): A SOC engagement issues a formal opinion, whereas consulting engagements provide recommendations without an opinion. Wrong (D): SOC engagements provide examination-level assurance with an opinion, however agreed-upon procedures engagements are limited to reporting findings.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: easy / L2 — SOC for Cybersecurity broader audience
(5, 'SOC Engagements', 'Lakeside Insurance''s board of directors needs independent assurance on the company''s overall cybersecurity posture to share with regulators and business partners. No specific outsourced service is the focus. Which engagement type is designed for this broader audience?', '["SOC for Cybersecurity engagement report", "SOC 1 Type II restricted-use report", "SOC 2 Type II restricted-use report", "SOC 3 Trust Services general-use report"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): Per the AICPA SOC for Cybersecurity guide, this engagement is designed for boards of directors, analysts, regulators, and business partners who need assurance about an organization''s entity-wide cybersecurity program. Unlike SOC 2, which focuses on a specific service, SOC for Cybersecurity covers the entire cybersecurity risk management program. Wrong (B): SOC 1 focuses on controls relevant to user entities'' financial reporting rather than entity-wide cybersecurity. Wrong (C): SOC 2 evaluates controls over a specific service, whereas Lakeside needs entity-wide cybersecurity assurance not tied to any particular service. Wrong (D): Although SOC 3 is general-use, it summarizes a SOC 2 report on a specific service rather than covering entity-wide cybersecurity.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Evaluating post-remediation reporting obligations
(5, 'SOC Engagements', 'Sentinel Payment Systems undergoes a SOC 2 Type II engagement for the period April 1 through March 31. In December, the service auditor identifies that access reviews were not performed for September and October. Management completes retroactive access reviews and requests the auditor to exclude the gap from the report. What is the appropriate auditor response?', '["Exclude the gap since management has retroactively remediated the deficiency", "Report the access review gap for September and October as an exception during the period", "Issue two separate reports covering the periods before and after remediation", "Suspend the engagement until access reviews are current for three consecutive months"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): Per AICPA SOC 2 guidance, the service auditor must report exceptions that occurred during the examination period. Although management remediated the deficiency, retroactive corrections do not eliminate the fact that controls were not operating effectively during September and October. The exception must be reported as it occurred. Wrong (A): The auditor cannot exclude documented exceptions from the report, whereas the report must reflect control effectiveness throughout the entire period. Wrong (C): Issuing two separate reports is not the appropriate response. Rather than splitting the engagement, the auditor reports exceptions within the single examination period. Wrong (D): There is no requirement to suspend the engagement. However, the auditor must evaluate the impact of the exception on the overall opinion.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Fair presentation and material omission impact
(5, 'SOC Engagements', 'Westbrook Data Solutions provides cloud storage services and uses a third-party encryption service for all data at rest. During a SOC 2 engagement, the service auditor discovers the encryption service is not mentioned in the system description. Management states the omission was unintentional. How should the auditor proceed?', '["Accept the omission since encryption is a technical detail not relevant to the system description", "Independently add the encryption service to the system description for completeness", "Request management to update the description and assess whether the omission affects fair presentation", "Withdraw from the engagement due to management''s failure to disclose material information"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): Per AICPA SOC 2 guidance, the service auditor evaluates whether the system description is fairly presented. An omitted encryption service that handles all data at rest is material to the system boundary, and the auditor should request management to correct the description rather than modifying it independently. Wrong (A): An encryption service handling all data at rest is a significant component, whereas technical details central to the system cannot be excluded from the description. Wrong (B): Preparing the system description is management''s responsibility. Unlike operational tasks, the auditor evaluates but does not prepare the description. Wrong (D): Although the omission is significant, withdrawal is not required. However, the auditor should assess whether the corrected description is fairly presented before proceeding.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: hard / L3 — Distinguishing Confidentiality from Privacy criteria selection
(5, 'SOC Engagements', 'Northstar Research processes proprietary pharmaceutical formulas for biotech clients and also maintains a database of clinical trial participant records including names and medical histories. The company is scoping its SOC 2 engagement. Beyond the mandatory Security criterion, which additional criteria are most relevant?', '["Availability and Processing Integrity for system uptime and accuracy", "Processing Integrity and Confidentiality for accuracy and trade secrets", "Confidentiality for proprietary formulas and Privacy for participant records", "Privacy and Availability for personal data and system accessibility"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): Per the AICPA Trust Services Criteria, Confidentiality protects information designated as confidential such as proprietary pharmaceutical formulas, whereas Privacy addresses personal information such as clinical trial participant records with names and medical histories. Both criteria are needed for Northstar''s distinct data types. Wrong (A): Availability and Processing Integrity address system uptime and processing accuracy rather than protecting confidential business information or personal data. Wrong (B): Although Confidentiality covers the proprietary formulas, Processing Integrity addresses processing accuracy rather than personal information like participant records requiring the Privacy criterion. Wrong (D): While Privacy covers participant records, Availability addresses system accessibility rather than protecting proprietary pharmaceutical formulas requiring the Confidentiality criterion.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Type II procedures beyond Type I
(5, 'SOC Engagements', 'Cornerstone Benefits Administration completed a SOC 1 Type I engagement last year and is now undergoing its first SOC 1 Type II engagement. The engagement partner explains that additional procedures will be performed beyond those in the prior Type I. What is the primary additional requirement?', '["Testing that controls operated effectively over the six-to-twelve month period", "Auditing the service organization''s annual financial statements", "Evaluating the service organization''s five-year strategic plan", "Assessing the user entities'' internal control environments"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): Per SSAE 18 / AT-C 320, a Type II engagement requires testing operating effectiveness of controls over a period, whereas a Type I engagement only evaluates whether controls are suitably designed at a point in time. This operating effectiveness testing is the primary distinction between the two engagement types. Wrong (B): SOC engagements are attestation engagements rather than financial statement audits. Neither Type I nor Type II requires auditing financial statements. Wrong (C): Strategic plan evaluation is not part of any SOC engagement, unlike the control testing required by attestation standards. Wrong (D): The service auditor tests the service organization''s controls, however user entities are responsible for their own internal control environments and CUECs.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L2 — Service organization definition and examples
(5, 'SOC Engagements', 'Harmon Technology operates data centers that host applications for 60 client companies. Each client''s internal controls depend on the physical security, environmental protections, and access controls Harmon maintains at these facilities. Under AICPA SOC guidance, how is Harmon classified?', '["As a user entity because it uses technology infrastructure", "As a service organization because it provides services relevant to clients'' internal controls", "As a subservice organization because it only provides hosting", "As an internal audit function of each client company"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): Per AICPA SOC guidance, a service organization is any entity that provides services to user entities where those services are relevant to the user entities'' internal controls. Harmon operates data centers that directly affect its clients'' control environments, making it a service organization rather than a user entity. Wrong (A): Harmon is the provider of services, not the consumer. A user entity is the organization that uses the service organization''s services, whereas Harmon delivers them. Wrong (C): A subservice organization is a third party used by the service organization itself. Although Harmon provides hosting, it is the primary service organization in this relationship. Wrong (D): Harmon is an independent entity, unlike an internal audit function which operates within a single organization.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Three elements of management assertion for Type II
(5, 'SOC Engagements', 'Beacon Cloud Services is finalizing its management assertion for a SOC 2 Type II engagement covering July 1 through June 30. The service auditor asks management to confirm that the assertion addresses all required elements. What must management''s written assertion cover for a Type II engagement?', '["Compliance with GAAP, accuracy of financial statements, and adequacy of disclosures", "Organizational profitability, customer satisfaction scores, and employee retention rates", "Fair presentation of the system description, suitable design of controls, and operating effectiveness during the period", "IT budget adherence, vendor contract compliance, and technology roadmap alignment"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): Per AICPA SOC 2 guidance under AT-C 205, management''s Type II assertion addresses three elements: the system description is fairly presented, controls are suitably designed to meet applicable Trust Services Criteria, and controls operated effectively throughout the examination period. Unlike a Type I assertion, the Type II assertion includes operating effectiveness. Wrong (A): GAAP compliance and financial statement accuracy relate to financial statement audits rather than SOC engagement management assertions. Wrong (B): Profitability, satisfaction, and retention are business metrics, whereas management''s SOC assertion addresses the system description and controls. Wrong (D): Although IT budget and vendor compliance may be operationally relevant, they are not the three elements required in a SOC management assertion.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — SOC 2 Availability criterion definition
(5, 'SOC Engagements', 'Which Trust Services Criterion in a SOC 2 engagement evaluates whether systems meet committed uptime and accessibility requirements?', '["Security criterion addressing access controls and threats", "Processing Integrity criterion addressing transaction accuracy", "Confidentiality criterion addressing restricted information", "Availability criterion addressing system accessibility and uptime"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct (D): Per the AICPA Trust Services Criteria, the Availability criterion evaluates whether the system is accessible for operation and use as committed or agreed upon, including uptime, disaster recovery, and business continuity. Unlike other criteria, Availability specifically addresses system accessibility commitments. Wrong (A): The Security criterion addresses access controls and threat protection rather than system uptime and accessibility commitments. Wrong (B): Processing Integrity evaluates whether transactions are processed completely and accurately, whereas Availability focuses on system accessibility. Wrong (C): Confidentiality addresses protection of restricted information, however Availability specifically covers system uptime and accessibility requirements.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Carve-out implications for user entity auditor
(5, 'SOC Engagements', 'Pinnacle Payroll Services uses the carve-out method in its SOC 1 report for a subservice organization that manages its disaster recovery site. A user entity auditor wants to rely on Pinnacle''s SOC 1 report for the payroll processing audit but also needs assurance over disaster recovery. What must the user entity auditor do?', '["Perform separate procedures to evaluate the subservice organization''s disaster recovery controls", "Rely solely on Pinnacle''s SOC 1 report since it references the subservice organization", "Require Pinnacle to switch to the inclusive method for the next engagement", "Issue a qualified opinion on the user entity''s financial statements due to the carve-out"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): Per AICPA SOC 1 guidance, the carve-out method excludes the subservice organization''s controls from scope. Unlike the inclusive method, which covers both organizations in one report, the carve-out requires the user entity auditor to separately evaluate the subservice organization''s controls through its own SOC report or direct testing. Wrong (B): The carve-out method specifically excludes the subservice organization''s controls, whereas a mere reference does not provide the assurance the user entity auditor needs. Wrong (C): The user entity auditor cannot require the service organization to change its methodology. However, the auditor can request a separate SOC report from the subservice organization. Wrong (D): A carve-out does not automatically result in a qualified opinion. Rather than qualifying, the auditor performs additional procedures to obtain sufficient assurance.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Evaluating SOC for Cybersecurity vs SOC 2 for vendor management
(5, 'SOC Engagements', 'A healthcare system''s vendor risk management team currently requires all critical IT vendors to provide SOC 2 Type II reports. A consultant recommends switching to SOC for Cybersecurity reports to simplify vendor oversight. The team should consider which key limitation before making this change?', '["SOC for Cybersecurity reports are more expensive and take longer to produce", "SOC for Cybersecurity covers entity-wide cybersecurity but may not address controls specific to the services each vendor provides to the healthcare system", "SOC for Cybersecurity reports cannot be shared with regulators or external parties", "SOC for Cybersecurity reports do not require a CPA to perform the engagement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): Per the AICPA SOC for Cybersecurity guide, this engagement evaluates an organization''s overall cybersecurity risk management program but is not scoped to specific services. However, the healthcare system needs assurance over controls directly relevant to each vendor''s specific services, which SOC 2 provides through service-specific Trust Services Criteria evaluation. Wrong (A): Cost and timing are not the key limitation. In contrast, the fundamental issue is scope: entity-wide cybersecurity versus service-specific controls. Wrong (C): SOC for Cybersecurity reports can be shared with regulators and external parties, whereas this statement mischaracterizes the report''s distribution. Wrong (D): SOC for Cybersecurity engagements must be performed by a CPA, unlike this claim which incorrectly suggests otherwise.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — SOC 2 restricted-use distribution vs SOC 3
(5, 'SOC Engagements', 'Why are SOC 2 reports classified as restricted-use rather than general-use?', '["Because they expire after 90 days and must be renewed", "Because they contain detailed control descriptions and test results that are sensitive", "Because they are only valid for organizations with fewer than 500 employees", "Because they must be filed with the SEC before distribution"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): Per AICPA SOC 2 guidance, SOC 2 reports contain detailed information about control descriptions and testing results, making them restricted-use reports limited to management, regulators, and specified parties. In contrast, SOC 3 reports omit these details and are classified as general-use. Wrong (A): SOC 2 reports do not have a 90-day expiration, whereas the restriction relates to the sensitive detail in the report content. Wrong (C): The restricted-use classification applies regardless of organization size, unlike this claim which incorrectly ties distribution to employee count. Wrong (D): SOC 2 reports are not filed with the SEC. Although distribution is restricted, the restriction comes from AICPA guidance rather than a regulatory filing requirement.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
